chapters.push({id:'design',week:'8–10',title:'Designing and testing solutions',intro:'Turn a problem into a working solution you can justify. Develop a club-order manager through investigation, design, development and evaluation.',objectives:['Write measurable requirements and a realistic schedule.','Design modular algorithms using a structure chart and stubs.','Select tests, debug systematically and evaluate evidence against requirements.'],sections:[
['Investigate: understand the problem',`<p>A school club sells badges for $3 each. Its organiser wants a console program to record orders, calculate totals, save records and summarise the session. The problem is keeping calculations and records consistent without repeatedly doing them by hand.</p><p>Ask what an order contains, which inputs are valid, where records should be saved and what the organiser needs to see. For this learning project, use a synthetic order ID and quantity. Do not collect real student names or payment details.</p><table><tr><th>ID</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Acceptance evidence</th></tr><tr><td>R1</td><td>Accept a non-negative whole-number quantity; reject unsuitable input and allow retry.</td><td>Try letters, a decimal string, a negative integer and zero.</td></tr><tr><td>R2</td><td>Calculate badge cost at 300 cents per badge.</td><td>Quantities 0, 1 and 4 give 0, 300 and 1200 cents.</td></tr><tr><td>R3</td><td>Store the quantity of each accepted order in a session list.</td><td>Enter 2, 0 and 5; confirm three elements in the correct order.</td></tr><tr><td>R4</td><td>Append each accepted order as ID, quantity and cost to a text file.</td><td>Check the file after two runs; previous records must remain.</td></tr><tr><td>R5</td><td>Display the session's total quantity, total cost and first largest-order index, or a clear empty-session message.</td><td>Check typical, tied-maximum and empty sessions.</td></tr></table><p>Agree whether zero quantity is permitted: this brief permits it. Order IDs must be non-empty synthetic labels without commas or line breaks so the simple record format remains unambiguous. State assumptions such as an available writable project folder.</p>`],
['Plan the work and dependencies',`<p>A schedule records tasks, dependencies and checkpoints. Design the record format before writing file code; implement calculations before testing them. Allow time to fix failures. A Gantt chart shows tasks against time; the shaded cells below indicate the planned working period.</p><table><tr><th>Task</th><th>Week 8</th><th>Week 9</th><th>Week 10</th></tr><tr><td>Requirements, schedule and design</td><td>■</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Functions and file integration</td><td></td><td>■</td><td>■</td></tr><tr><td>Testing and corrections</td><td>■ Design tests</td><td>■ Unit tests</td><td>■ Whole-system tests</td></tr><tr><td>User trial and evaluation</td><td></td><td></td><td>■</td></tr></table><p>Ten of the twelve lessons across weeks 8–10 are project lessons: four in week 8, two in week 9 and four in week 10. The other two teach API concepts and debugging/attribution in context. Record actual progress alongside the plan and explain changes.</p>`],
['Design: divide the solution into modules',`<p>A structure chart describes how tasks are divided and called. It is not a flowchart: its branches represent module relationships rather than every decision or repetition.</p><pre>main\n├── read_order_id → order_id\n├── read_quantity → quantity\n├── order_cost(quantity) → cost_cents\n├── save_order(order_id, quantity, cost_cents)\n└── summarise_orders(quantities) → summary</pre><p>Keep one logical task per module. Pass data through parameters and return results where useful. The mainline coordinates the work; it should not contain every detail. A stub reserves a function's name and interface before its implementation exists.</p><pre>def save_order(order_id, quantity, cost_cents):\n    # Stub: deliberately unfinished.\n    raise NotImplementedError("Implement file append here")</pre><p>This stub stops with an explicit error if called. It is a design aid, not a completed solution. Replace it deliberately and test the implementation before using it.</p><pre>quantities = empty array\nWHILE organiser chooses to add an order\n    read and validate order ID and quantity\n    cost = order_cost(quantity)\n    append record to file\n    append quantity to quantities\nEND WHILE\nIF quantities is empty THEN\n    output empty-session message\nELSE\n    output totals and first maximum position\nEND IF</pre><p>Writing the file before adding the quantity to the session avoids reporting a saved order if the write fails. Decide how a write failure is reported and how the user can retry. Walk through the design using test data before coding.</p>`],
['Develop: build a small working part',`<p>Begin with a calculation function that has no input prompts or file side effects. This makes it easy to test independently. Use integer cents for this fixed-price example. Convert to dollars only when formatting output.</p><p>Next add validation, then file storage, then session processing. Run a small test after each change. Keep meaningful names, constants, comments that explain decisions, and consistent indentation. Save dated versions or use a teacher-approved version-control tool; keep a backup outside the working copy.</p><p>An API is an interface through which software requests another component's behaviour. The built-in file interface is one example; importing <code>csv</code> would use a standard-library module through its documented functions. A web API is another kind, not the only kind. You do not need a live web service for this project. Follow documented inputs, outputs and error behaviour, and acknowledge reused code.</p>`],
['Test: choose evidence that can expose mistakes',`<p>Unit testing checks one module in isolation. Integration testing checks that parts exchange and save data correctly. User acceptance testing asks whether the solution meets the agreed needs. Choose expected results independently before running each test.</p><table><tr><th>Test</th><th>Input / action</th><th>Expected result</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td>T1</td><td>Quantity -1</td><td>Reject; retry allowed</td><td>R1</td></tr><tr><td>T2</td><td>Quantity 0</td><td>Accept; cost 0 cents</td><td>R1–R2</td></tr><tr><td>T3</td><td>Quantity 1</td><td>Cost 300 cents</td><td>R2</td></tr><tr><td>T4</td><td>Text "three" or "2.5"</td><td>Reject without an unhandled conversion error</td><td>R1</td></tr><tr><td>T5</td><td>Orders 2, 0, 5</td><td>Quantity total 7; cost 2100 cents; largest index 2</td><td>R3, R5</td></tr><tr><td>T6</td><td>Orders 5, 5</td><td>First largest index 0</td><td>R5</td></tr><tr><td>T7</td><td>No orders</td><td>Clear message; no access to index 0</td><td>R5</td></tr><tr><td>T8</td><td>Save one order, restart and save another</td><td>Both records retained with separate lines</td><td>R4</td></tr></table><p>The lower valid boundary is zero: test -1, 0 and 1. Exercise both decision branches and loop cases with zero, one and several orders. Add tests for order-ID format and write failure. Record actual output, pass/fail, correction and retest result; a table of expected outputs alone is not evidence that tests ran.</p>`],
['Debug: isolate the first incorrect state',`<p>Classify the failure. A syntax error might be a missing colon. A runtime error might be division by zero or an invalid index. A logic error might use write mode instead of append mode, losing earlier records without crashing.</p><p>Reproduce the smallest failing case. Trace values by hand, step through the code or temporarily print the relevant variables. Compare each intermediate state with your prediction. Correct the cause, then rerun the failed test and earlier tests to check for regressions.</p><p>For maximum-position logic, keep a trace table with current index, current quantity and remembered best index. If tied values unexpectedly choose the last order, inspect whether your comparison uses greater-than-or-equal instead of strict greater-than.</p>`],
['Evaluate: judge the result against the brief',`<p>Ask a classmate acting as organiser to attempt a short acceptance script: create an order, recover from unsuitable input, check the saved record and request a summary. Observe the attempt rather than silently guiding every action.</p><p>For each requirement, cite a test or observation and state met, partly met or not met. Explain limitations and a practical next improvement. “It works well” is not an evaluation; “R4 passed T8, but a read-only folder still causes an unhandled error” identifies both evidence and a limitation.</p><p>Finish with a developer retrospective: what helped, what delayed progress, which design decision you would change and how your next schedule would improve. A change after evaluation may lead back to design and development; the process can repeat.</p><p>Keep a source log for borrowed code, media and documentation. Acknowledgement does not replace licence permission. Describe your own contribution and any permitted assistance; use the school's assessment rules if this learning project becomes a graded task.</p>`],
['Project pack and checkpoints',`<p><a class="button" href="project-starter.py" download>Download unfinished project starter</a> <a class="button secondary" href="project-workbook.md" download>Download project workbook</a></p><p>The starter contains explicit unfinished functions and is not a reference solution. Use the workbook for requirements, a schedule, design decisions, test records and evaluation. Teacher checkpoints should review the student's reasoning and observed progress, not just the final file.</p><table><tr><th>Week</th><th>Checkpoint</th><th>Bring evidence</th></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Design review</td><td>Requirements, schedule, structure chart, pseudocode and predicted tests</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Build review</td><td>Working modules, unit-test results and a record of corrections</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Demonstration and evaluation</td><td>Program, synthetic data file, completed tests, acceptance evidence and retrospective</td></tr></table><p>This is a textbook learning project. Its checkpoints are not extra weighted assessments. Your teacher must separately set any assessment conditions, rubric, authentication and submission requirements.</p>`]
],code:'BADGE_PRICE_CENTS = 300\n\ndef order_cost(quantity):\n    if not isinstance(quantity, int) or isinstance(quantity, bool):\n        raise ValueError("Quantity must be a whole number")\n    if quantity < 0:\n        raise ValueError("Quantity cannot be negative")\n    return quantity * BADGE_PRICE_CENTS\n\ndef format_money(cents):\n    return f"${cents // 100}.{cents % 100:02d}"\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    for quantity in [0, 1, 4]:\n        actual = order_cost(quantity)\n        expected = quantity * 300\n        print(quantity, actual, expected, format_money(actual))\n',practice:[['Improve','Rewrite the requirement “the program should be easy to use” so you can test it.','One possible criterion: after an invalid quantity, display a specific message and let the organiser retry without restarting. Observe this with the input three followed by 2.'],['Design','Why put calculation in a function separate from input and file storage?','You can supply known inputs and check returned values without typing or changing files. A failed calculation test can be isolated from input and storage problems.'],['Test','Why are -1, 0 and 1 useful quantity tests?','They are below, at and above the lower valid boundary. A check that accidentally rejects zero will be exposed.'],['Evaluate','All calculation tests pass, but a second run replaces the first record. Is the solution complete?','No. R4 fails. Investigate the file mode, switch to append if appropriate, then rerun the persistence and earlier tests.']],task:'Implement the club-order manager from your own design. Submit the code, synthetic data file and project workbook. Demonstrate invalid-input recovery, two-run persistence, an empty session and a tied maximum. Keep scope to a console application that fits the ten allocated project lessons.',solution:'# Illustrative unit-test fragment, not the complete project solution.\ndef order_cost(quantity):\n    if not isinstance(quantity, int) or isinstance(quantity, bool) or quantity < 0:\n        raise ValueError("Invalid quantity")\n    return quantity * 300\n\nassert order_cost(0) == 0\nassert order_cost(1) == 300\nassert order_cost(4) == 1200\ntry:\n    order_cost(-1)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("Negative quantity correctly rejected")\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError("Negative quantity was accepted")\nprint("Calculation checks passed")\n',recap:'Build from measurable requirements. Design modules and tests before coding, gather actual evidence, isolate failures and evaluate each requirement with a clear limitation or improvement.',lessons:['Week 8, lesson 1: agree requirements and acceptance criteria.','Week 8, lesson 2: plan dependencies and the Gantt chart.','Week 8, lesson 3: design modules, data and pseudocode.','Week 8, lesson 4: desk-check the design and review the checkpoint.','Week 9, lesson 1: learn API contracts and code/source attribution.','Week 9, lesson 2: practise error classification and debugging.','Week 9, lesson 3: implement and test calculation/input modules.','Week 9, lesson 4: implement storage and review build evidence.','Week 10, lesson 1: integrate the session summary.','Week 10, lesson 2: execute tests, correct and retest.','Week 10, lesson 3: conduct the organiser acceptance trial.','Week 10, lesson 4: demonstrate, evaluate and write the retrospective.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/unittest.html',refTitle:'Python unit testing reference (optional extension)',quiz:{q:'Which is evidence that a solution meets a requirement?',options:['A prediction of the output alone','A recorded actual result compared with a pre-defined expected result','A large amount of code'],answer:1,explanation:'A completed comparison provides test evidence. A prediction is only the test plan.'}});
